artifact.self_contained_problem:
Titan, Saturn's largest moon, possesses a dense nitrogen-dominated atmosphere with roughly 1.5 bar surface pressure and a few percent methane (CH4) at the surface. Photochemical and escape modeling indicates that atmospheric CH4 is irreversibly destroyed by ultraviolet-driven photolysis and conversion to higher hydrocarbons (which are then deposited on the surface), with a characteristic depletion timescale of order a few times 10^7 years — far shorter than the ~4.5 Gyr age of the Solar System. Determine, with quantitative justification:

(a) When (in absolute time before present) and by what physical/chemical mechanism Titan's present atmosphere — and in particular its methane component — was emplaced. Distinguish among the following hypotheses and identify which (if any) is correct:
   (i) the atmospheric methane is continuously or episodically resupplied (e.g., from a subsurface reservoir such as clathrates, a hydrocarbon-rich interior, serpentinization, or cryovolcanism), so that the steady-state CH4 inventory is geologically young;
   (ii) the present CH4 is the terminal remnant of a much larger primordial inventory that has been monotonically declining over Solar System history;
   (iii) the present CH4 was injected into the atmosphere by a relatively recent (≪ 1 Gyr) crustal-destabilization or outgassing event.

(b) Show how measurements of isotopic ratios in atmospheric and surface species (in particular D/H, 12C/13C, 14N/15N, and 36Ar/40Ar), combined with global inventories of liquid and solid hydrocarbons on the surface and in the near subsurface, can be used to place a quantitative bound on the age of the current atmosphere, and derive such a bound.
